# Line endings.
#
# `* text=auto` lets git normalise to LF in the repository while checking out
# whatever the platform prefers. That is the right default, but it breaks one
# thing here, so that one thing is pinned below.
* text=auto

# packages/contracts-artifacts/dist is a TRACKED build product (see the negation
# in .gitignore). Its freshness guard — scripts/check-dist-fresh.mjs — re-runs the
# export and asks git whether anything changed.
#
# The export always writes LF. With `text=auto`, a Windows checkout rewrites the
# working copy to CRLF, so the very next re-export differs from the checked-out
# file on every single line and the guard fails on a clean clone. Pinning eol=lf
# keeps the working copy byte-identical to what the export produces, which is the
# only way the comparison means anything.
packages/contracts-artifacts/dist/** text eol=lf

# Same reasoning, different consumer: the golden RPC fixture is compared byte-wise
# by the session engine's replay client, which throws on any unrecorded request.
packages/session-engine-old/test/fixtures/*.json text eol=lf

# Solidity and lockfiles are LF everywhere by convention; forge and pnpm both
# write LF and neither expects to see it changed underneath them.
*.sol text eol=lf
pnpm-lock.yaml text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF. A CRLF checkout breaks the shebang under WSL and
# Linux ("bad interpreter"), and secrets-bundle.sh is the first thing a second
# machine runs -- before there is any environment in which to debug it.
*.sh text eol=lf
